//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The x86 column of the combo run (#319): a CPU host, admitted against the same
// way every accelerator is. Every other file in fleet/ models an accelerator --
// m4-uma.vx included, which is the Apple NPE's memory rather than an arm64 host
// -- so until this one existed a program could be admitted against an H100 and
// not against the machine compiling it (#341).
//
// This is the build box the campaign already uses, so every figure below can be
// re-read from the hardware rather than transcribed -- with one exception, which
// is worth knowing before anyone tries.
//
// THE INTERCONNECT FIGURE CANNOT BE MEASURED ON THE EC2 INSTANCE. The part is
// genuinely two sockets, and a c4.8xlarge reports two NUMA nodes whose CPU lists
// match the `lscpu` quoted below exactly. The nodes are not backed by physical
// locality. Measured on one, 2026-09-11:
//
//   - all four (cpu node, memory node) pairs read at 98 GB/s within 1%, and
//     pointer-chase at 117-119 ns within 1.4%
//   - binding to one node measures the same as interleaving across both
//   - both are impossible if the nodes were real: one socket peaks at 68.3 GB/s
//     and QPI at 38.4, so neither a 98 GB/s single-node read nor a 96.7 GB/s
//     remote read can be what it claims to be
//
// `/proc/PID/numa_maps` confirms `--membind` puts every page on the bound node,
// so the usual check passes while the thing it checks is not real: the guest is
// reporting its own page accounting, not the hypervisor's placement.
//
// So the 38.4 GB/s below stays UNVERIFIED until someone runs
// `utils/campaign/run_numa_probe.sh` on bare metal. The probe now refuses a
// machine like this rather than reporting a ratio from it.
//
// A CPU is the *other* degenerate case, and more degenerate than m4-uma. There
// the host and device share one physical memory; here the device is the host, so
// a `transfer` to `Memory::HBM` moves data from DRAM to the same DRAM. What the
// model still has to get right is the hierarchy underneath: the last-level cache
// is real, and a tile that fits in it is worth knowing about. A model that says
// nothing sensible about a machine whose seam costs nothing will not be trusted
// about a machine whose seam costs everything.
//
// `HBM` and `L2` keep the fleet's shared vocabulary (fleet/README.md): one
// program text, every SKU, swap the flag. On this part the memory is DDR4 and
// there is no HBM anywhere near it -- the name says which role the space plays,
// not what it is made of, and a last-level cache is a last-level cache whether
// it is called L2 or L3.
//
// `SMEM` is absent, because a scratchpad is not a role this machine has anything
// to play. See the note where it would have been.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// ONE NUMA domain, not the whole box. This part is two sockets, and `lscpu`
// reports node0 = CPUs 0-8,18-26 and node1 = 9-17,27-35, so the 60 GiB is two
// 30 GiB domains with a real cost between them.
//
// `HBM` is node0's share, and `PEER_HBM` below is node1's. That is the same
// pair node-8gpu.vx uses between two GPUs, and it is the same relation: a tile
// is resident in one domain, and reaching it from the other crosses a link. The
// fleet's names say which role a space plays rather than what it is made of, so
// "the other domain of the same kind, across a link" is `PEER_HBM` here exactly
// as it is there.
//
// spec: `/proc/meminfo` MemTotal = 61768396 kB (58.9 GiB) on the build box, and
// the AWS C4 specification for the instance this part identifies (36 vCPU / 60
// GiB; the E5-2666 v3 is the custom Haswell-EP part built for that family).
// 30 GiB per node is that 60 GiB halved, which is what `numactl -H` reports as
// the per-node size.
//
// 60 GiB is declared rather than the 58.9 GiB the kernel reports, per
// fleet/README.md: declared capacity is the device, not the deployment budget.
// The ~1.1 GiB difference is firmware and kernel reservation, and it is the same
// class of gap as the reserved fraction of a GPU's HBM.
//
// UNVERIFIED bandwidth, and derived rather than quoted: the E5-2600 v3 family
// has 4 DDR4 channels per socket at up to 2133 MT/s, so
//   4 channels x 2133 MT/s x 8 B = 68.3 GB/s per socket.
// That is one socket's memory controllers' peak, not an achievable copy rate,
// and it is per node because a node is one socket. The previous 136 GB/s here
// was both sockets' controllers quoted against a space that a single tile has to
// fit in, which over-stated a single domain's rate by 2x as well as its size.
// Nothing rests on it until measured; see the seam note below.
Memory HBM {
  capacity: 30 GiB, bandwidth: 68 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// node1's share. A space of its own rather than more of `HBM`, because that is
// the whole content of NUMA: the bytes are reachable, and reaching them costs
// something that reaching local bytes does not.
//
// spec: as `HBM` above -- the two sockets are the same part.
Memory PEER_HBM {
  capacity: 30 GiB, bandwidth: 68 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// The last-level cache, which on this part is L3 rather than L2 -- the fleet's
// `L2` names the role (last level on die), not the level number.
//
// spec: `lscpu -C` on the build box -- L3, ONE-SIZE 25M, 20-way, 2 instances
// (one per socket), 64 B line. 25 MiB is declared rather than the 50 MiB both
// sockets hold, because a tile is resident in one socket's LLC; the other
// socket's copy is a coherence problem, not extra capacity.
//
// UNVERIFIED bandwidth: Intel publishes no L3 bandwidth figure for this part.
// 200 GB/s is a placeholder of the right order for a ring-interconnect LLC and
// no claim rests on it.
Memory L2 {
  within: Memory::HBM, capacity: 25 MiB, bandwidth: 200 GB/s, managed: cached, scope: device
}

// There is no `SMEM` here, and that is the point rather than an omission.
//
// The fleet's third space is a *scratchpad*: per-SM shared memory, allocated
// because a program put something there. x86 has nothing of the kind. The
// nearest thing is the per-core L2 -- 256 KiB, 8-way, one per core by
// `lscpu -C` -- and that is a cache. A tile "placed" in it is resident only
// while the hardware has no better use for the lines, under pressure from every
// other access in flight.
//
// Declaring it anyway, as a cached space of 256 KiB, would keep the shared
// vocabulary complete and would be a category error: a program that places a
// tile in `Memory::SMEM` would be *admitted* against this machine and would have
// been told something false, because nothing here guarantees residency. The
// capacity check would pass on a number that does not mean what it means on
// every other SKU.
//
// Leaving it out makes the compiler say what is true:
//
//   Error: Cannot transfer from CPUDRAM to Custom("SMEM"): no hardware path
//   exists
//
// A program that wants a scratchpad cannot have one here, and finds out at
// compile time. That is the whole claim this fleet exists to support, and it
// would be odd to weaken it to keep a table tidy.
//
// The per-core L2 is still worth modelling one day -- a tile that fits in it
// really is faster -- but under a name that says "cache", not one that says
// "scratchpad".

// The degenerate seam, taken further than m4-uma's. There the host and the
// device share one physical memory and a transfer is a memcpy; here the device
// *is* the host, and `Memory::CPU_DRAM` and `Memory::HBM` name the same DRAM.
//
// UNVERIFIED, and declared at one node's 68 GB/s peak for the reason m4-uma.vx
// records: a memcpy reads and writes, so it moves two bytes of traffic per byte
// copied and cannot exceed half the peak even in principle. The model should
// over-predict this edge by at least 2x, and if a measurement says otherwise the
// harness is wrong rather than the hardware surprising.
//
// The QPI hop is the edge this file exists to make sayable. UNVERIFIED, and
// derived rather than quoted: Haswell-EP carries QPI 1.1 at 9.6 GT/s, 2 bytes
// per transfer, and this part has two links between the sockets, so
//   9.6 GT/s x 2 B x 2 links = 38.4 GB/s per direction.
//
// Declared in both directions because they are separately measurable and there
// is no reason yet to believe they agree. Against the 68 GB/s a node reaches its
// own memory at, the model therefore predicts a remote access costs 1.8x a local
// one -- which is a number to go and falsify, not a result. Measuring it is the
// first NUMA experiment; nothing else in this file rests on it.
Topology Device {
  // spec: Intel Haswell-EP, as a compute target rather than a host.
  arch: x86_64,
  memory: Memory::HBM,
  visible: [Memory::HBM, Memory::PEER_HBM, Memory::L2],
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::HBM : 68 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::L2,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::PEER_HBM : 38 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::PEER_HBM -> Memory::HBM : 38 GB/s
}
